// `Bool` — an ordinary prelude union, not a primitive (ruling #147,
// `spec/decisions-ml-dialect-bool-2026-07.md`).
//
// This declaration is real, compilable prelude source on the `Seq.hex` model, and
// that choice is load-bearing: because the `derives` clause here is the *ordinary*
// derivation door, `Eq<Bool>`, `Ord<Bool>`, `Show<Bool>`, and `Hash<Bool>` are derived
// instances rather than compiler-provided ones. Collections Part 2 §4.4 bans smuggling
// a `Hash` instance into stdlib source; `Bool` obeys that ban rather than excepting it
// — even the prelude obtains `Hash` only through the door users use.
//
// Constructor order is normative and is `False | True` (Haskell's order): derived `Ord`
// follows declaration order (Unions §7), so this order is what makes `False < True`
// without a hand-written instance, and it is also the order JS `<` on booleans already
// has, which the representation pin relies on.
//
// The compiler grants this one declaration exactly one privilege — the representation
// pin (decisions doc §3): a `Bool` is the JS `boolean`, `True` emits `true`, `False`
// emits `false`, and `Bool` is `boolean` in emitted `.d.ts`. That is the single
// exception to the all-nullary string rule (Unions §6.2), it is unavailable to any user
// declaration, and the compiler verifies this file's shape rather than trusting it. The
// pin is also what licenses `and`/`or`/`not`/`implies`/`iff` and `if`/`while` conditions
// to eliminate a `Bool` without `match` (Operators §4, §11) — against the string
// representation, `&&` on `"False"` would be truthy nonsense.
export union Bool derives (Eq, Ord, Show, Hash) =
    | False
    | True
